+| `waypoint` | `questMark`, `drawFx` | a diamond over the compass's mark (the compass's own diamond takes its colour) - coloured by its kind (the quest its look; a pin sky blue, a destination purple, the exit teal-green, Okabe-Ito colours a colour-blind player tells apart, each with its word before the distance) at its own spot on the floor, in perspective, with the distance; dim behind a wall; an edge arrow when it is off to a side (F7; Shift+F3 cycles its look: gold, ivory, cyan, magenta, large) |
 | `guide-trail` | `questMark`, `drawFx` | gold embers along the way to the compass's mark - the tracked quest, or a destination a module asked the compass for - planned round walls over the floor (F4 on/off, Shift+F4 its look): the embers are a pure function of a path and the clock; pools lie on the floor as translucent stains and sparks rise additively, so the trail reads on snow as well as in a dungeon |
 
 | `pins` | `mapPick`, `questMark`, `drawFx` | a right click on the local map sets the destination (any spot, or a quest's ringed building); F2 drops a pin where the crosshair meets the world, F3 cycles the compass quest -> the exit -> places -> quest, Shift+F2 removes the followed one; arriving removes it; a jump of 150 m (a new place, its own frame) clears them - but not the open world's tile step (819.2 m, the frame moving as it streams: the places move with it), nor a teleporter's jump (landing at the far end the map has just recorded, `world.known()`). The exit and a dungeon map's pin keep their floor and are reached on it alone. A waypoint followed on the region map (`world.waypoints()`) is led to outdoors: one followed anew takes the compass, the rest wait in F3's cycle. THE EXIT is off until the player pins it: a right click on the dungeon map's exit mark (`mapPick`'s `exit`), or near where they came into the place (kept silently, never shown; the loader's route, where the map cannot say), pins it and the compass, waypoint and trail lead out; it stays in F3's cycle, its flag clears it, a new place forgets it. No key of its own. It asks the game to mark the place (`retarget`), so the compass, waypoint and trail follow it with no module reading another |
